WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.464
This briefing comes from Identity and
Directory, the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:09.137
Workbook's book on the move from
Active Directory to Entra ID.

4
00:00:09.137 --> 00:00:13.885
What this book accomplishes is a directory
where an object's place in the tree has

5
00:00:13.885 --> 00:00:18.562
become attributes and rules, and every
service account has an owner and a target.

6
00:00:18.562 --> 00:00:20.351
Of the workbook's four tracks,

7
00:00:20.351 --> 00:00:24.674
it carries the directory side of the
HR track and the service accounts.

8
00:00:24.674 --> 00:00:29.500
People's attributes are in HR-Driven
Identity, devices and servers

9
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.237
in Devices and Endpoints, and AI
agents in AI Identity Governance.

10
00:00:34.237 --> 00:00:38.297
We'll take it through four
questions: why identity matters now,

11
00:00:38.297 --> 00:00:41.799
what changes, who owns which
decisions, and how to do it.

12
00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:44.762
The short version: this
isn't a directory upgrade.

13
00:00:44.762 --> 00:00:47.975
It decides whether every
later control actually works.

14
00:00:47.975 --> 00:00:49.600
Let's start with why.

15
00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.605
Why does identity matter so much now?

16
00:00:54.605 --> 00:00:58.855
Because federal Zero Trust policy
moved where access gets decided.

17
00:00:58.855 --> 00:01:04.421
M-22-09 requires enterprise-managed
identities and phishing-resistant MFA.

18
00:01:04.421 --> 00:01:10.136
NIST 800-207 removes the trust that
used to come from being on the network:

19
00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:12.468
every request is evaluated on its own.

20
00:01:12.468 --> 00:01:17.155
And CISA's maturity model puts
identity first among its five pillars.

21
00:01:17.155 --> 00:01:21.442
In practice, the identity provider
enforces whatever it believes.

22
00:01:21.442 --> 00:01:24.117
Who is this person,
and is that still true?

23
00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:26.906
Which groups and roles
do they hold, and why?

24
00:01:26.906 --> 00:01:28.905
Is their device managed and healthy?

25
00:01:28.905 --> 00:01:32.709
Conditional Access, Privileged
Identity Management and

26
00:01:32.709 --> 00:01:36.355
device compliance all read
those facts, on every sign-in.

27
00:01:36.355 --> 00:01:37.969
So if the facts are wrong,

28
00:01:37.969 --> 00:01:41.680
every policy built on them is
wrong too, at machine speed.

29
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.020
That's why this move decides whether every
later control has true facts to act on.

30
00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:55.533
Microsoft isn't retiring Active Directory;
it still supports and develops it.

31
00:01:55.533 --> 00:01:59.607
But every road now points to the
cloud, and some of the dates are real.

32
00:01:59.607 --> 00:02:04.265
NTLM is deprecated, and its
first version is already

33
00:02:04.265 --> 00:02:08.707
gone from Windows 11 24H2
and Windows Server 2025.

34
00:02:08.707 --> 00:02:13.233
Entra ID now blocks sync from taking
over privileged cloud accounts.

35
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:15.775
And the date to circle is April 7, 2027:

36
00:02:15.775 --> 00:02:20.184
by then every Connect Sync server
has to be on a current version

37
00:02:20.184 --> 00:02:24.932
with application-based authentication,
or synchronization stops.

38
00:02:24.932 --> 00:02:29.660
Behind the dates is a direction:
Cloud Sync replacing Connect Sync,

39
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.669
and cloud authentication instead of AD FS.

40
00:02:32.669 --> 00:02:34.570
The trap is lift and shift.

41
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:40.117
Sync the forest as it stands, and twenty-five
years of organizational units (OUs),

42
00:02:40.117 --> 00:02:44.470
stale accounts and undocumented
trusts arrive in the tenant.

43
00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.656
Meanwhile the governance that hierarchy
carried, who could manage what and where

44
00:02:49.656 --> 00:02:54.670
policy applied, gets left behind, because
Entra ID has no OU tree to carry it.

45
00:02:58.420 --> 00:02:59.557
So what changes?

46
00:02:59.557 --> 00:03:03.120
These are two different models,
not two versions of one product.

47
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.808
Active Directory is a hierarchy:
a forest, domains, and nested OUs.

48
00:03:08.808 --> 00:03:10.661
Entra ID is one flat tenant,

49
00:03:10.661 --> 00:03:15.295
where structure comes from
attributes and administrative units.

50
00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:18.720
In AD, much of who you are is
where you sit in the tree.

51
00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.306
In Entra ID, it should come
from attributes supplied by an

52
00:03:22.306 --> 00:03:25.968
authoritative source, and for
the workforce that means HR.

53
00:03:25.968 --> 00:03:30.088
Policy moves from Group Policy to
Conditional Access and Intune,

54
00:03:30.088 --> 00:03:31.995
targeted by those attributes.

55
00:03:31.995 --> 00:03:36.821
The protocols change, standing Domain
Admin gives way to just-in-time roles,

56
00:03:36.821 --> 00:03:41.257
and management moves to Microsoft
Graph; the slide lays out each pair.

57
00:03:41.257 --> 00:03:43.595
But the key idea is the bottom line.

58
00:03:43.595 --> 00:03:47.832
In Active Directory, governance is
implied by where an object sits.

59
00:03:47.832 --> 00:03:51.047
In Entra ID it has to
be stated explicitly,

60
00:03:51.047 --> 00:03:54.710
as data and rules, or it
simply doesn't exist.

61
00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:02.278
Before the size of the job, it
helps to know which job is whose,

62
00:04:02.278 --> 00:04:05.785
because one O U tree holds
four different populations.

63
00:04:05.785 --> 00:04:08.510
The workbook treats them as four tracks.

64
00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:12.702
People, the HR track: this book
builds the directory side,

65
00:04:12.702 --> 00:04:16.348
and HR-Driven Identity
supplies the attributes.

66
00:04:16.348 --> 00:04:20.960
End-user devices and servers, the Intune
and Arc tracks: this book's Group

67
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:25.885
Policy analysis finds their settings,
and Devices and Endpoints moves them.

68
00:04:25.885 --> 00:04:30.774
And the non-person track: every service
account gets an owner and a target,

69
00:04:30.774 --> 00:04:34.685
here in this book, with AI agents
in AI Identity Governance.

70
00:04:34.685 --> 00:04:38.116
Appendix A maps that work
to account management,

71
00:04:38.116 --> 00:04:44.035
attribute-based access and least
privilege in NIST 800-53, and to M-22-09.

72
00:04:44.035 --> 00:04:47.240
Next, everything else
the directory holds up.

73
00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.498
The second thing that changes
is the size of the job.

74
00:04:53.498 --> 00:04:56.522
Most migration plans
count users and groups.

75
00:04:56.522 --> 00:05:01.944
But Active Directory has also been
holding the rest of the estate together:

76
00:05:01.944 --> 00:05:07.628
DNS and DHCP, the certificate services
behind smart cards, Wi-Fi, VPN and servers,

77
00:05:07.628 --> 00:05:11.213
RADIUS sign-in, applications
that bind over LDAP,

78
00:05:11.213 --> 00:05:16.460
the sync engine itself, domain-joined
devices, time, and file permissions.

79
00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:18.460
You don't need to memorize the list.

80
00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:22.598
The point is that none of it moves
just because users sync to the cloud.

81
00:05:22.598 --> 00:05:27.123
Some interim steps, like the
NPS extension for Entra MFA,

82
00:05:27.123 --> 00:05:30.173
are useful, but they still depend on AD.

83
00:05:30.173 --> 00:05:33.986
So the program gives each
dependency its own adapter:

84
00:05:33.986 --> 00:05:37.886
an owner, a target, a cutover
test and a validation check.

85
00:05:37.886 --> 00:05:41.260
And they retire in priority
order, not all at once.

86
00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:43.150
Next, delegation.

87
00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.938
The third change is delegation.

88
00:05:48.938 --> 00:05:53.549
In Active Directory, who could manage
what was mostly expressed through OUs.

89
00:05:53.549 --> 00:05:58.400
Entra ID has none, so delegation
has to be rebuilt on purpose.

90
00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:02.263
The program uses three tiers:
tenant-wide control at Tier 0,

91
00:06:02.263 --> 00:06:06.127
service administration like
Exchange or Intune at Tier 1,

92
00:06:06.127 --> 00:06:11.600
and departmental work like the help desk
at Tier 2, scoped to administrative units.

93
00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.274
The platform has real limits,

94
00:06:13.274 --> 00:06:16.412
and the old design documents
got several of them wrong.

95
00:06:16.412 --> 00:06:21.173
Administrative units can't be nested,
role-holding groups can't be dynamic,

96
00:06:21.173 --> 00:06:25.387
and restricted management has to
be chosen when a unit is created.

97
00:06:25.387 --> 00:06:29.291
That last one also takes its
users out of Entra ID Governance,

98
00:06:29.291 --> 00:06:32.726
so no access reviews or
lifecycle workflows for them.

99
00:06:32.726 --> 00:06:35.542
So the program's default
is unrestricted units,

100
00:06:35.542 --> 00:06:40.890
with restricted management a deliberate,
recorded choice for sensitive accounts.

101
00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.978
One more thing changes, and users
will feel it: how they sign in.

102
00:06:48.978 --> 00:06:55.666
M-22-09 requires phishing-resistant MFA,
and it names PIV as the simplest route.

103
00:06:55.666 --> 00:06:57.653
That flips the usual roadmap.

104
00:06:57.653 --> 00:07:00.764
Older plans saved smart-card
sign-in for last.

105
00:07:00.764 --> 00:07:03.968
But federal staff already
carry PIV or CAC cards,

106
00:07:03.968 --> 00:07:08.531
so certificate-based authentication
in Entra ID is the fastest path:

107
00:07:08.531 --> 00:07:13.015
the credential's already issued,
and no federation server is needed.

108
00:07:13.015 --> 00:07:17.822
The book runs four waves: privileged
accounts first, then card holders, then

109
00:07:17.822 --> 00:07:23.327
managed Windows devices with Windows Hello,
and finally everyone else with passkeys.

110
00:07:23.327 --> 00:07:26.399
Along the way, SMS and
voice codes go for staff,

111
00:07:26.399 --> 00:07:30.965
and so do legacy protocols that
send a password with every request.

112
00:07:30.965 --> 00:07:36.165
Those are found in the sign-in logs, fixed,
blocked, and watched for thirty days.

113
00:07:36.165 --> 00:07:38.670
One thing stays on: password hash sync,

114
00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:42.420
for leaked-credential
detection and as a fallback.

115
00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:46.770
Who.

116
00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:51.195
Directory migrations fail more often
on ownership than on technology,

117
00:07:51.195 --> 00:07:54.645
so the book separates who
decides from who does the work.

118
00:07:54.645 --> 00:07:58.528
Deciding: the CIO and sponsor
set scope, funding and sequence;

119
00:07:58.528 --> 00:08:01.168
the CISO sets the control requirements;

120
00:08:01.168 --> 00:08:05.516
and the authorizing official
accepts any risk the program carries

121
00:08:05.516 --> 00:08:10.020
instead of removing, like a service
account that can't be converted yet.

122
00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.205
Building and running: identity
engineering builds the target,

123
00:08:14.205 --> 00:08:19.678
from sync and authentication to PIM and
administrative units; AD administrators

124
00:08:19.678 --> 00:08:25.070
clean up the source and eventually retire
it, and the ISSO assembles the evidence.

125
00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:28.264
Then come the people
who supply and accept:

126
00:08:28.264 --> 00:08:31.732
HR, application owners,
the help desk and users.

127
00:08:31.732 --> 00:08:33.782
But one owner is usually missing.

128
00:08:33.782 --> 00:08:36.758
Microsoft's tools each own
a slice of the picture,

129
00:08:36.758 --> 00:08:41.145
and nobody owns the gaps between them
unless the program names someone.

130
00:08:41.145 --> 00:08:43.130
Next, how it's done.

131
00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:50.137
The book's How chapters follow five steps:

132
00:08:50.137 --> 00:08:53.393
discover, assess, build,
migrate and prove.

133
00:08:53.393 --> 00:08:57.898
Discovery starts with the read-only
access every employee already has,

134
00:08:57.898 --> 00:09:02.480
and only then asks for narrow, time-bound
access to what you still can't see.

135
00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:05.291
The assessment covers
the whole forest without

136
00:09:05.291 --> 00:09:07.892
ever requesting rights
that expose secrets.

137
00:09:07.892 --> 00:09:12.924
For the build, the book recommends Cloud
Sync unless something needs Connect Sync,

138
00:09:12.924 --> 00:09:16.327
password hash sync everywhere,
PIM for every admin role,

139
00:09:16.327 --> 00:09:18.917
and a tested Conditional Access baseline.

140
00:09:18.917 --> 00:09:22.403
Migration runs in waves,
moving to phishing-resistant

141
00:09:22.403 --> 00:09:25.579
sign-in and switching off
legacy authentication.

142
00:09:25.579 --> 00:09:28.274
And proof means drift
checks and an evidence

143
00:09:28.274 --> 00:09:30.968
bundle the authorizing
official can rely on.

144
00:09:30.968 --> 00:09:35.876
A few rules hold every time: never
enforce an untested policy on everyone,

145
00:09:35.876 --> 00:09:38.657
keep two cloud-only break-glass accounts,

146
00:09:38.657 --> 00:09:43.360
and treat any closure as unproven
until it carries its evidence.

147
00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:50.756
Chapter 11 puts the steps together
in a reference deployment.

148
00:09:50.756 --> 00:09:56.217
It's a framing pattern, not a real engagement:
a civilian agency with two forests,

149
00:09:56.217 --> 00:10:00.300
a trust between them, and a
fleet of SQL Server instances.

150
00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:03.557
The figures are illustrative,
but the mechanics are real.

151
00:10:03.557 --> 00:10:09.587
It runs in five phases: discover,
normalize, map, migrate and validate.

152
00:10:09.587 --> 00:10:10.988
Two moments stand out.

153
00:10:10.988 --> 00:10:15.269
Every account the registry can't tie
to a real owner becomes a finding.

154
00:10:15.269 --> 00:10:18.378
And every change is recorded
with who asked for it,

155
00:10:18.378 --> 00:10:20.947
on what authority, and
a hash of the result.

156
00:10:20.947 --> 00:10:24.170
What comes out is an evidence
bundle for the authorizing

157
00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:27.813
official and the assessor, who
authorize the agency's own system.

158
00:10:27.813 --> 00:10:29.711
And it belongs to the agency.

159
00:10:29.711 --> 00:10:32.193
When the integrator leaves, the registry,

160
00:10:32.193 --> 00:10:36.792
the findings and the evidence stay
behind for the next authorization cycle.

161
00:10:36.792 --> 00:10:38.700
So, what are we asking for?

162
00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:43.400
Four asks.

163
00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:47.663
Name the owners, including someone
who owns the gaps between the tools.

164
00:10:47.663 --> 00:10:49.763
Start read-only discovery now.

165
00:10:49.763 --> 00:10:52.096
It needs no elevated access to begin,

166
00:10:52.096 --> 00:10:56.687
so any request for more can follow
the evidence instead of coming first.

167
00:10:56.687 --> 00:11:02.239
Put the dates on the plan: Connect Sync
has to be upgraded by April 7, 2027,

168
00:11:02.239 --> 00:11:08.749
and NTLM and legacy authentication should
come off wave by wave, not in one weekend.

169
00:11:08.749 --> 00:11:11.982
And agree what done means:
every control backed by

170
00:11:11.982 --> 00:11:15.450
evidence an assessor can read,
not a status in a ticket.

171
00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:19.750
Identity matters because it's now
where every access decision gets made.

172
00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:23.934
Getting it right once, while the
platform is being rebuilt anyway,

173
00:11:23.934 --> 00:11:27.188
costs far less than rebuilding
the same gaps twice.

174
00:11:27.188 --> 00:11:31.815
Identity and Directory has the full
argument and the runbooks, every claim

175
00:11:31.815 --> 00:11:34.427
footnoted, and HR-Driven Identity picks up

176
00:11:34.427 --> 00:11:37.188
where the directory's
facts should come from.

177
00:11:37.188 --> 00:11:38.010
Thank you.
